@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# Self-hosting with Kamal
+
+Fizzy's Kamal configuration is set up for a single Linux server with Docker, SQLite, local file storage, HTTPS from Kamal Proxy, and background jobs on the same server.
+
+## Before you deploy
+
+Install the Ruby version in `.ruby-version` and install the bundle with `bundle install`.
+
+1. Fork this repository and choose a public hostname for Fizzy. Point its DNS A/AAAA records to your server, and make sure SSH, HTTP (port 80), and HTTPS (port 443) are reachable. The HTTP and HTTPS ports are needed for Kamal Proxy's Let's Encrypt certificate. Docker must also be available on the machine where you run Kamal, since it builds the image locally.
+2. In `config/deploy.yml`, replace `fizzy.example.com` with that hostname.
+3. In the same file, set `SMTP_ADDRESS` and `SMTP_DOMAIN` for your mail provider. The defaults assume authenticated SMTP on port 587 with STARTTLS; adjust `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`, and `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` if your provider requires different settings. Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` to an address accepted by that provider.
+4. Copy `.kamal/secrets.example` to `.kamal/secrets` and replace the placeholders. Generate a new Rails secret with `bin/rails secret` and fill in your SMTP credentials. `.kamal/secrets` is ignored by Git. Kamal uses a local registry for this one-server setup, so no Docker Hub account or registry token is needed.
+
+If your SSH account is not `root`, set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`; it must be able to run Docker. The builder is set to `amd64` by default, so switch it to `arm64` for an ARM server.
+
+The server hostname in `config/deploy.yml` is also used as the SSH destination. If you SSH to a different address than the public app hostname, change the entry under `servers.web` to that address while keeping the `proxy.host` and `APP_HOST` set to the public hostname.
+
+## Deploy
+
+Run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal builds the image locally, transfers it to the server, configures Kamal Proxy, obtains an HTTPS certificate, and deploys Fizzy. Subsequent releases use:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The `fizzy` Docker volume is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It contains all four production SQLite databases as well as local Active Storage uploads, and is reused when Kamal replaces the app container during a deploy. Back up this volume separately; a Docker volume protects data from container replacement, not server or disk loss.
+
+Solid Queue runs through Puma in the app container (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true`), so no Redis service or separate worker server is needed. The web and job concurrency are set to one process each as a modest single-box starting point and can be tuned for the server's resources.
